`verilator_config
// Lint waiver list for this project.
//
// Every entry carries a one-line justification. An empty file is the goal; a
// growing file is a design smell. Waivers are reviewed at each phase gate.
//
// Current count: 8

// coh_pkg declares the complete parameter set up front, so parameters read only
// by modules that do not exist yet are flagged. Revisit at the Phase 12 gate:
// by then every parameter must have a consumer, and this waiver should be
// deletable. If it is not, the parameter is dead and should be removed instead.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDPARAM -file "*coh_pkg.sv"

// The address-decode helpers each extract one field from a full address, so by
// construction every one of them leaves the other fields unread. Making this
// warning go away would mean passing pre-sliced arguments, which is exactly the
// magic-number-at-the-call-site problem the helpers exist to prevent.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*coh_pkg.sv"

// SYNCASYNCNET fires on every module that resets flops with `negedge rst_n` and
// also writes `disable iff (!rst_n)` in an SVA property: the same net is then
// seen as both an async reset and a synchronous condition. That combination is
// the standard assertion idiom, not a reset-domain bug. The invariant the rule
// actually protects -- that every always_ff uses the same async reset, or is
// explicitly annotated as unreset -- is enforced directly by
// scripts/check_style.sh check 3, which is stricter than this rule.
lint_off -rule SYNCASYNCNET

// PINCONNECTEMPTY fires where an instance deliberately leaves an output
// unconnected by writing `.count_o()`. The alternative -- omitting the pin
// entirely -- hides the fact that the output exists, and connecting it to a
// dummy signal just moves the complaint to UNUSEDSIGNAL. Writing the empty
// connection explicitly is the most readable of the three, and it is checked by
// review: an output that should have been used is a design error the reader can
// see, whereas an omitted pin is invisible.
lint_off -rule PINCONNECTEMPTY

// CMPCONST fires in route_compute at the mesh edges. With MESH_X = MESH_Y = 2
// a coordinate is one bit wide, so at MY_X = 1 the test `dst_x_i > my_x` is
// constantly false -- there is no "further east" to route to. The comparison is
// the correct general expression of dimension-ordered routing and stops folding
// the moment the mesh is wider than two; rewriting it to suit a 2x2 would make
// the module wrong for every other size. The property that actually matters --
// that nothing is ever routed off the edge -- is checked directly by the
// a_no_*_escape assertions in noc_top rather than inferred from this warning.
lint_off -rule CMPCONST -file "*route_compute.sv"

// l1_cache instantiates l1_coh_fsm four times -- for the core request, the
// victim being evicted, an incoming forward and an incoming response -- and
// each instance consumes only the subset of l1_action_t that its event class
// can produce. The same is true of the message struct: a VN1 forward carries
// no data and a VN2 response carries no requester. Wiring per-instance action
// subtypes would multiply the type definitions and, worse, split the protocol
// table across several structs; the table is the thing that has to stay
// readable in one piece. That the unused bits really are unreachable rather
// than unimplemented is what test_protocol_l1_table.py checks, cell by cell,
// which is a stronger statement than this warning makes.
// Phase 12 review item: re-check that no action bit is unused at EVERY
// instance, which would mean the action exists in the table and nowhere else.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*l1_cache.sv"

// dir_ctrl latches a whole coh_msg_t as the request under service, and the
// ack_count field of an incoming request is meaningless -- the directory is
// the one that computes AckCounts, never a consumer of them. Splitting the
// message into request and response subtypes to avoid the warning would mean
// two struct definitions, two packetization paths in the NIC, and a conversion
// at every boundary, to save seven bits of register.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*dir_ctrl.sv"

// tile_nic carries whole flits and whole messages through paths that do not
// consume every field: an incoming flit's destination coordinates were already
// used by the router that delivered it, an outgoing message's own dst is
// encoded into the flit header rather than read again, and the two-way VN2
// arbiter's grant is used one-hot so only one bit is read. Narrowing the types
// per path would mean a struct per direction and a conversion at every
// boundary, which is a worse trade than this waiver.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*tile_nic.sv"
